1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,568
How many ways into the locked room should there be?

2
00:00:02,618 --> 00:00:05,064
The room has two kinds of visitor.

3
00:00:05,114 --> 00:00:09,240
Buyers' browsers come from the internet, to type a card into the card frame.

4
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:13,704
And services from other units, like ecom, come from their own servers.

5
00:00:13,754 --> 00:00:19,488
One big proxy that opened every connection would see every caller's traffic in clear text.

6
00:00:19,538 --> 00:00:22,416
That box would be the most valuable box in the bank.

7
00:00:22,466 --> 00:00:26,064
So the room has two narrow doors, and nothing else listens.

8
00:00:26,114 --> 00:00:28,584
Think of a bank counter and a mail slot.

9
00:00:28,634 --> 00:00:34,512
The counter serves the public: it opens your envelope, and handles only a few kinds of request.

10
00:00:34,562 --> 00:00:42,864
The mail slot serves partners: it reads only the address on a sealed envelope, and drops it, still sealed, into the right box.

11
00:00:42,914 --> 00:00:46,800
The first door is the payers' door, and it is the counter.

12
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:52,104
It opens the browser's encrypted connection, with a certificate that browsers trust.

13
00:00:52,154 --> 00:00:55,056
It passes on only the card frame's few paths.

14
00:00:55,106 --> 00:00:57,144
Every other path gets not found.

15
00:00:57,194 --> 00:01:02,760
It also limits how fast one payer can submit a card, but never how fast a page loads.

16
00:01:02,810 --> 00:01:05,352
Lesson 13 comes back to that limit.

17
00:01:05,402 --> 00:01:09,072
The second door is the callers' door, and it is the mail slot.

18
00:01:09,122 --> 00:01:15,168
A caller's connection arrives encrypted, with the name of the service it wants written on the outside.

19
00:01:15,218 --> 00:01:20,040
The door reads only that name, and passes the connection on, still sealed.

20
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:23,592
That is pass-through: the door never opens what it carries.

21
00:01:23,642 --> 00:01:29,400
The service behind it checks the caller's certificate and token itself, as Lesson 7 showed.

22
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:35,040
Only four names pass: the card vault, the two adapters, and the room's login server.

23
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:38,640
Any other name, or no name at all, is refused.

24
00:01:38,690 --> 00:01:43,152
So no door ever holds every caller's traffic in clear text.

25
00:01:43,202 --> 00:01:45,192
Now look at the mail slot's rules.

26
00:01:45,242 --> 00:01:49,776
It reads the name before anything else, and looks it up in one short list.

27
00:01:49,826 --> 00:01:56,544
Each name points to one service, and the last line sends everything else nowhere, because nothing listens there.

28
00:01:56,594 --> 00:01:58,608
Now check the counter on your laptop.

29
00:01:58,658 --> 00:02:02,856
Ask the payers' door for the card frame's script, and it answers 200.

30
00:02:02,906 --> 00:02:07,224
Ask it for a path inside the card vault, and it answers 404.

31
00:02:07,274 --> 00:02:14,088
On the laptop the door listens on port 8443; a server uses the usual port, 443.

32
00:02:14,138 --> 00:02:19,464
A counter that handles only the frame, and a mail slot that never opens the post.

33
00:02:19,514 --> 00:02:24,072
Next: how a caller gets its certificate without ever handing over its key.
